[agent] Final contents: **exactly 400 rows, one per `row_id`, each the latest revision** — confirmed in both the offline store (`count`=400, distinct keys=400) and the online store (400 rows). Spot-checks on corrected rows are correct, e.g. R00000 → `dormant`/1770103920000 (its latest, not the older `active`/1770056220000), R00009/R00023 likewise.
[agent] ### How the latest-per-key revision was computed (all platform-side)
[agent] The 3 batches (490 rows, 400 distinct `row_id`s, out of order) were combined and loaded. A key finding about this CLI/backend:
[agent] - The **offline store appends every distinct revision** (it retains 490 rows for history) — it does **not** reconcile to latest-by-event-time on the primary key.
[agent] - The **online store's upsert uses `event_time` as the precombine** — loading all batches produced exactly one row per `row_id` = the max-`updated_at` revision, regardless of insert order.
[agent] I used that platform-computed deduplication as the source of truth: read the 400 reconciled rows back from the online store, then rebuilt the FG from just those rows so that **both** the offline and online stores contain exactly one latest row per `row_id`. The dedup/latest-selection was performed by the platform (the online event-time precombine); no joins/transforms were computed locally — local steps were only file concatenation and serializing the platform's already-deduplicated output to CSV for re-ingest (the JSON read path mis-infers epoch-ms as `timestamp`, so CSV was required to keep `updated_at` as `bigint`).
[agent] ### Interface note (for tooling improvement)
[agent] The `hops` CLI has no direct primitive to collapse an append-loaded offline feature group to latest-per-key by event time: `fg insert` appends/over­writes without event-time reconciliation on the offline side, `fg derive` only joins (no aggregation/dedup), and `hops sql` (Trino/iceberg) does not see cached-FG offline tables. Achieving an offline "latest per key" table required round-tripping through the online store's event-time upsert. A first-class "upsert to latest by event_time" or SQL-over-offline-FG capability would make this backfill pattern a single step.
